# kidnix read-aloud settings. Read by kidnix-piper.service (systemd
# EnvironmentFile syntax: KEY=value, no shell expansion, no quotes needed).
#
# This is the one file to edit to change the child's voice. Everything else
# about read-aloud -- the speech-dispatcher module, the rate mapping, the
# espeak-ng fallback -- follows from what is set here.
# Rationale and measurements: docs/spikes/tts.md.

# The voice model. Three ship; this line picks one. Changing it takes effect on
# the next `systemctl --user restart kidnix-piper.service`, or the next login --
# no rebuild, no network.
#
#   en_GB-alba-medium.onnx   63 MB on disk, ~114 MB resident, ~25-50 ms per
#                            short utterance. A Scottish-accented female voice.
#                            THE DEFAULT.
#
#   en_GB-cori-high.onnx    114 MB on disk, ~168 MB resident, ~110-300 ms.
#                            A different speaker (southern UK English female).
#                            This was the default until 2026-08-23.
#   en_GB-cori-medium.onnx   64 MB on disk, ~114 MB resident, ~25-50 ms.
#                            The same speaker as cori-high, smaller and faster.
#
# WHY ALBA. cori was chosen on licence grounds alone -- it is public domain, so
# it was the only en_GB voice that cost no attribution -- at a point where
# nobody involved had heard any of the candidates. Someone then listened, and
# cori was judged bad enough to change. Alba is CC-BY-4.0; the credit that buys
# it is at /usr/share/licenses/kidnix-voices/ATTRIBUTION and in the ledger at
# docs/LICENSES.md 6. Both cori tiers stay in the image precisely so that this
# is a reversible opinion and not a rebuild: if alba grates too, put the cori
# line back.
#
# Every voice here is 22,050 Hz mono, so nothing downstream needs touching when
# this line changes. Ten candidates were rendered saying the shell's own lines
# in output/tts-samples/ if the argument is ever reopened.
KIDNIX_PIPER_MODEL=/usr/share/kidnix/voices/en_GB-alba-medium.onnx

# Seconds of silence between sentences *inside one utterance*. Piper's own
# default is 0.2, which is adult-paced.
#
# 0.25 rather than the 0.35 this file shipped with, because the pause a child
# actually hears between two sentences is not this number. The shell says its
# two-sentence lines through SpeechManager.speak_then(), which leaves its own
# SENTENCE_GAP_MS = 400 ms between them (shell/kidnix_shell/speech.py) -- so
# 0.35 here stacked on top of that and made a two-clause line drag. This value
# only bites within a single utterance, e.g. "The sun is going down. Finish
# this one, or one last little thing?", where the sentence is already 10%
# longer than piper's default because of the length_scale below.
#
# If a child is losing the second half of two-sentence lines, raise this to
# 0.35-0.40 (docs/research/06 5.1 and 7.5 #30 ask for 300-500 ms for a
# pre-reader) -- and change SENTENCE_GAP_MS with it, not instead of it.
KIDNIX_PIPER_SENTENCE_SILENCE=0.25

# Seconds of no speech after which the server exits and gives the memory back.
# Socket activation restarts it on the next utterance at the cost of one model
# load (~250-380 ms). Set to 0 to stay resident for the whole session.
KIDNIX_PIPER_IDLE_EXIT=900

# Set to 1 to make piper log its real-time factor for every utterance to the
# journal. Useful when measuring on new hardware, noisy in normal use.
KIDNIX_PIPER_DEBUG=0

# --- knobs that are deliberately NOT in this file ----------------------------
#
# SPEAKING PACE (piper's --length_scale) is not set here, because it is not a
# per-machine setting: it is per *utterance*, and it comes from
# speech-dispatcher's rate. kidnix-piperd maps it as
#
#     length_scale = 1.0 - (speechd rate / 200)      quantised to 0.05
#
# so, in the shipped configuration:
#
#     rate   0  (a client with no opinion)      -> 1.00   piper's own pace
#     rate -10  (DefaultRate, /etc/speech-dispatcher/speechd.conf) -> 1.05
#     rate -20  (SPEECH_RATE, what the shell asks for)             -> 1.10
#     rate -60  (never asked for today)                            -> 1.30
#
# 1.05-1.15 is the band that sounds unhurried to a five-year-old without
# sounding like a slowed-down recording; the shell sits at 1.10 and calm mode
# can go slower. To change the pace for everything on the machine, move
# DefaultRate in speechd.conf; to change it for the child only, move
# SPEECH_RATE in shell/kidnix_shell/access.py. Do NOT reach for it by editing
# length_scale here: the shell would keep overriding you.
#
#   (Until 2026-08-23 none of that worked at all. The generic module's
#   GenericRateMultiply is read as hundredths, so `1` meant 0.01 and every
#   rate collapsed to 0 -- the image spoke at length_scale 1.000, adult pace,
#   for its whole life. Fixed in
#   /etc/speech-dispatcher/modules/kidnix-piper.conf; the story is in
#   docs/spikes/tts.md 8.)
#
# PITCH is not a knob. A VITS model sings at the pitch it was trained at;
# speech-dispatcher's pitch is passed through and piper ignores it.
#
# NOISE (--noise_scale 0.667, --noise_w 0.8) is left at piper's defaults on
# purpose. They are what the model was trained to sound like; turning them
# down makes the voice flatter and more monotone -- the exact quality people
# mean when they say a synthesiser "sounds like a robot" -- and turning them
# up makes it unstable. The only place kidnix sets them to 0 is the build-time
# determinism probe in build_files/65-tts.sh, where identical output is the
# whole point and nobody is listening.
#
# LOUDNESS is not a knob either. Piper's output is close to full scale
# (measured peak 1.00 on every voice tried), and the ceiling is the hardware
# mixer at 70% plus WirePlumber's soft mixer. speech-dispatcher's volume is
# honoured downwards only, in kidnix-piper-say.
